  "result": "I implemented `satisfies(version, range)` in `src/semver.js` following `docs/SPEC.md`, and `npm test` now passes (5 of 5).\n\nIt covers every section of the spec. It returns `false` for invalid versions and ranges, and it never throws. It handles very large version numbers.\n\nThe tests are in `test/satisfies.test.js`. They cover each spec table, the prerelease rules and ordering, and invalid or non-string input.\n\n**Spec gaps:** the spec doesn't say what to do in these cases, so I picked behaviour for them:\n- `>*` and `<*` match nothing.\n- `>=*` and `<=*` match everything.\n- Positions after a wildcard are ignored, so `1.x.3` is read as `1.x`.\n- A prerelease on a partial version, such as `1.x-beta`, is invalid.\n- An empty `||` segment, as in `1.0.0 ||`, means \"any version\".\n- Leading zeros are accepted in prerelease identifiers, because the spec only forbids them in `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`.\n\nMy first test run had one failure, which was a mistake in my own test: I treated `'x'` as an invalid range, but it's a valid wildcard. I fixed the test, not the implementation.",
